<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<component type="desktop-application">
  <id>tech.redfoxlabs.Thirties</id>
  <metadata_license>CC0-1.0</metadata_license>
  <project_license>GPL-3.0-or-later</project_license>

  <name>Thirties</name>
  <summary>Plan your day with an assistant</summary>
  <description>
    <p>
      Thirties is a personal assistant for your time. It gathers your tasks and calendar into one place, lays the day out as 48 half-hour “thirties” anchored to sunrise and sunset, and lets you plan, re-plan and review the day in plain conversation, on your desktop or from your phone.
    </p>
    <p>Features include:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A day canvas of thirties, with daylight and dark from your location</li>
      <li>Planning by conversation: “put the walk at 4:00”, “it went until 4:30”</li>
      <li>Your Joplin lists and GNOME calendars in one plan; it can tick off and add Joplin items for you</li>
      <li>Reminders and check-ins, on the desktop and by Telegram</li>
      <li>Rolling unfinished items into the next day, and learning how long things really take</li>
      <li>An assistant that runs privately on your computer (Gemma); it isn't ready yet, so for now Thirties runs in debug mode on Gemini</li>
      <li>Sync between computers through a folder you choose</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This is an early alpha.</p>
  </description>

  <developer id="tech.redfoxlabs">
    <name>Matthew Samson (Red Fox Labs)</name>
  </developer>

  <url type="homepage">https://github.com/snakeeyes021/thirties</url>
  <url type="vcs-browser">https://github.com/snakeeyes021/thirties</url>
  <url type="bugtracker">https://github.com/snakeeyes021/thirties/issues</url>

  <translation type="gettext">thirties</translation>
  <launchable type="desktop-id">tech.redfoxlabs.Thirties.desktop</launchable>
  <content_rating type="oars-1.1" />

  <branding>
    <color type="primary" scheme_preference="light">#e9d4bb</color>
    <color type="primary" scheme_preference="dark">#7a5434</color>
  </branding>

  <releases>
    <release version="0.4.0" date="2026-11-15" type="development">
      <description>
        <p>One conversation on the desktop and the phone, what's next without asking, a schedule that keeps to what you said, a day that makes way for what you're really doing, the days ahead in pencil, Month and Year in the Timeline, projects that move a piece at a time, and help with people, goals, reading and watching.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The app's chat and Telegram are one conversation: the same messages, in the same words, in both. The morning message is in the day it opens, and Telegram commands and their replies show in the app too</li>
          <li>The Timeline has Month and Year tabs, and the Week shows what each block holds. Its marks, ticks and quarters are easier to read, and a card left Not reported can still be ticked there</li>
          <li>On the Agenda, one tap says work started or finished, and another logs how much energy you have. An item with no time yet can be done, given a time or put off, on the Agenda and the Timeline</li>
          <li>The days ahead are pencilled in: a later day shows what's planned for it on the Agenda and the Timeline, and its items can be done early, given a time or put off</li>
          <li>All conversations, by day, with a search, and spell check in the message box</li>
          <li>The night (your own sleep window) is a thin strip under the day in the Timeline; it opens when you click it or drag something into it, and you can plan in it like any other time</li>
          <li>An empty stretch that's already over takes a record of what happened then: it's saved as done, at the times you give, and counts in your statistics; it's never asked about or carried over</li>
          <li>Start notices and check-ins end with what's next when there's something new to say, including free stretches of an hour or more and today's items with no time yet. In Telegram, the commands are in the menu, /now and a What's next button show it whole, and a tick in the day panel reaches the phone too</li>
          <li>Once an evening, when two or more things are still not marked and there's room tonight, one offer to move them to tonight; a yes or a thumbs up does it</li>
          <li>Asked to stop adding what's next, the list of what's not marked yet, or the line saying which block you're in, Thirties stops, from now on or for today only; Preferences has the same three switches</li>
          <li>Every message is plain lines: nothing already done is listed unless you ask what got done, and a list item is always named, never shown as a code</li>
          <li>The schedule holds your own items, each once, and nothing comes off it unless you ask. An item you put on a later day stays off today until you bring it up</li>
          <li>Before a reply goes out, what it says is checked against the plan: a move it mentions has to be on the plan, only what's finished is marked done, and a question changes nothing</li>
          <li>Started isn't done: an item is done when you say it's finished. What you say you're doing now, or a rest you ask for, takes its time, and what's in the way moves</li>
          <li>Anything you ask for today gets a time on today's plan, including an item added to a list for today and one pinned to the day</li>
          <li>Free time stays free unless you fill it, and when the day is full Thirties says where things go instead of offering to drop them</li>
          <li>Asked to choose, Thirties chooses and says why; told to stop doing something, it stops; asked why something is where it is, it answers from what it has on record</li>
          <li>The morning says where each item kept from before went: "today at 3:00 PM", or "not today, later this week"</li>
          <li>Say you need quiet until something's over, and Thirties holds everything it would say by itself until then; reminders go out after, and a short line says messages are back on</li>
          <li>A reminder can carry details, and says them when it goes off. When something else is on after an item ends, the check-in asks whether you've started it</li>
          <li>Working longer on something counts as progress: whatever it pushed aside moves later instead of being marked missed, and a project's pieces are planned together</li>
          <li>Every project gets a small piece offered each day, and a part that ran long two days out of three gets more time the next time</li>
          <li>A project's backlog stays off your day: a ticket from it, or a line written like one, goes on the plan only when you name it</li>
          <li>A routine already on the plan moves when you place it again, and on a crowded day a later part of it goes at its usual fallback time. A step done late one day doesn't change where it goes the next</li>
          <li>Say you're still doing something and it stretches to when you'll really finish, with what follows moving along only as far as it has to. Give it a length and it's marked done by itself</li>
          <li>Pushing the day back moves only what has to move: meals stay close to their times, fixed events stay put, and the half hour after work stays clear. Meals are spaced from when you really ate</li>
          <li>Say you're off work and Work comes off those days</li>
          <li>An item can keep its own rules: nothing may overlap it, it depends on someone else, someone else sets its day and time, you have to set off for it by a certain time, or another item always follows it</li>
          <li>Say how you feel and how much time you have, and what's offered fits: lighter things on a low day, bigger ones when you have energy to spare</li>
          <li>When work ends, by the Agenda's button or by answering "Done with work?" with a yes or a time, the rest of the day is planned from then, and you hear how many daylight and dark thirties are left, with a few things that fit</li>
          <li>The week ahead is pencilled in, and each morning that day's pencilled items become its plan</li>
          <li>Parts of a project, epics to plan and report by, your own estimates in the unit you choose (set from an item's card too), and chores someone else at home does</li>
          <li>After three misses in a row, or three put-offs, Thirties offers to break the item into smaller pieces, and offers its first piece once it has them. Before today has a Break It Down button for it too</li>
          <li>Menus leave one-off items out, Thirties can make a new list when you ask, you choose how a to-do note with no checklist is read, and done items can go to a note you pick. Preferences shows how each list is read</li>
          <li>Help writing new list items in your own style, matched to how you already write them</li>
          <li>Thirties reads the library export you save from Goodreads (My Books → Import/Export): choose its folder in Preferences, and the newest export there is your reading list. Preferences says how old it is</li>
          <li>When you finish a book in Thirties, one short reminder to mark it read on Goodreads and export again, at the end of that reply</li>
          <li>Ask for something to read or watch and Thirties picks from your lists, your Media Queue first, and says which list each one came from</li>
          <li>People to keep in touch with: tell Thirties when you saw, texted or called someone, and it nudges you when it's been a while</li>
          <li>Goals keep the day you set them, and their own words ("in 2–4 months", "by March 1") give a real deadline from that day</li>
          <li>Progress reports for a day or a week end with what was pushed back to the backlog</li>
          <li>Once a week, the lasting patterns in your statistics go into the profile's Observations, where you can read and edit them</li>
          <li>Someone else's events stay off your day unless you say otherwise. The first time one of their series comes up, Thirties asks once whether it's yours too, naming whose calendar it's on, and the answer holds for the whole series; a calendar you've said isn't yours three times isn't asked about again</li>
          <li>An event on your calendar and someone else's, or on a calendar the household shares, is on your plan once, marked shared. Tell Thirties whose a calendar is and it uses that name from then on. On the Agenda, other people's events sit under their times, with Hide for today, Not mine and Mine too in one menu</li>
          <li>The sun and the moon on request: the next sunrise, the moon's phase, and the next full and new moon, worked out for where you are</li>
          <li>A daily check of your theatre for rush tickets, off until you ask for it: tell Thirties the theatre and the page that lists its shows. Preferences has its switch, and one for the keep-in-touch nudges</li>
          <li>Thirties always works with the model and settings you chose: if its config file can't be read, reminders, check-ins, Telegram and sync don't start on stand-in choices until the file is fixed</li>
          <li>A note to the engineers ("note to the engineers: the check-in came twice") is kept for them to read and never acted on, and Preferences shows the notes you've left</li>
        </ul>
      </description>
    </release>
    <release version="0.3.0" date="2026-11-12" type="development">
      <description>
        <p>Plans across weeks, months and years, a day that sorts out what was missed, and safer, quieter daily use.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Nothing very sensitive goes to Gemini: passwords, PINs, keys and codes, card and bank numbers, ID numbers, and medication or diagnosis names are swapped for placeholders before a message leaves the computer, and put back in the reply, even if the assistant writes one slightly off; a placeholder never ends up in your lists in place of the real thing</li>
          <li>Private notes (tagged "private" in Joplin, or in a list named Medical, Health or Finance) go to Gemini as item titles only, never their contents</li>
          <li>Safer Joplin writes: a change never overwrites an edit you make in the note at the same moment, a change Joplin saved without answering is never applied twice, and a morning's tidy-up of Today that has to wait never runs on a later day</li>
          <li>Items keep who they are when you reword, reorder or move them in Joplin: their plans, Today links and history follow them; a finished copy of a repeated item no longer hides the next copy, and an item written again after it was done is a new item</li>
          <li>Anything you add for today gets a time on the plan at once, in the first free time that fits; only when you say so ("if there's time", "no rush", "don't schedule it") does it go without one, under a new If there's time section at the end of the Agenda, the schedule message and the Today note</li>
          <li>If there's time items can be done, scheduled in the next gap, or put off to tomorrow, later this week, or dropped; a full day puts new things there and says so; when a free gap opens, a notice already being sent may name one of them; and what's left at the day's end comes back the next morning for this week</li>
          <li>For developers testing Thirties' own on-device assistant (Gemma): under 8 GB of memory, or after the bigger model fails to fit, Preferences offers the smaller model (Gemma 4 E2B); it's downloaded, checked and tested before the bigger one is removed, and Thirties never switches by itself</li>
          <li>Model downloads come from a fixed release of each file, so a new download is exactly the build that was tested</li>
          <li>The first message of the day, if it goes out late because the computer was asleep, says the wake-up or sunrise time as past, never as the time now</li>
          <li>Windows and order you state for today's items ("before the 4:00 call", "only between 10 and 3") are kept wherever an item is placed, even before it's planned; a time picked for you moves to the nearest one that keeps them, and a time you name that would break one is asked about once</li>
          <li>Nothing is removed or rearranged in your lists, memory or calendars unless you ask outright: archiving an item, rewording, splitting or moving list lines, forgetting something the assistant remembers, rewriting a profile section, or changing a whole calendar. Otherwise Thirties does the gentle version (a drop, an added line, hiding just that event) and says so, or asks first</li>
          <li>Something you missed gets its next day by itself: the first day from tomorrow with room for it, never past its deadline, or today if it can't wait ("as soon as possible"); the morning lists each one ("Keep: …, tomorrow") so you can change it</li>
          <li>Something skipped or left unmarked again and again no longer comes back every morning: after each miss in a row it rests longer (a day, then two, then four, and longer after that). A due date that's near still brings it back, and so does having said it can't wait, up to the third miss. After the third, Before today asks once whether you still want it: keep it for this week, park it for later, or drop it</li>
          <li>Time on a project can have a budget for a week, month, quarter, year or several years ("3 sessions this month"); it trickles down to each week and day, the week plan picks the days with room, and a deadline pulls the sessions earlier; a missed session is made up before its period ends, or counts toward the next</li>
          <li>Long sessions go on days off first, and anything outdoors needs daylight for its whole length</li>
          <li>Items can be in season only in some months ("only in summer", "May to September", or "(summer)" in the item); out of season they wait, and a deadline outside the season is moved to the last day in it</li>
          <li>A missed deadline that repeats (monthly, quarterly, yearly, or "(quarterly)" in the item) moves to its next date, and the work for it is planned back from there, sized to how much it takes: what you said, or the assistant's estimate, which it shows so you can correct it</li>
          <li>The longer plans keep adjusting as things finish or slip, pulling work forward when there's room (someday items too, last of all), with a bigger look at each new week, month and year</li>
          <li>Someone else's events (show-only calendars) have their own section at the end of the day, Not yours today, with Hide for today and Not mine; hiding a whole calendar still needs you to say the calendar</li>
          <li>A calendar that can't be read no longer makes the day look free: it keeps what it showed last, or the Agenda, the assistant and the morning message say it couldn't be read; cancelled events and events marked free take no time</li>
          <li>On Telegram, editing a message that was already answered never repeats what it did: a typo fix passes quietly, and any other change gets a note to send it as a new message; after a restart the bridge carries on from the last message it read, and a reply that didn't get through is still sent if Telegram answers again within a few hours</li>
          <li>A message to Telegram is sent again only when it never got there: a slow answer no longer turns one message into many copies, a "too many requests" is waited out, and the same words never go out twice within ten minutes from the queue</li>
          <li>Muting check-ins only quiets what Thirties says by itself: a schedule you ask for still shows what's not marked yet</li>
          <li>Pausing every check-in takes asking for it ("stop the check-ins", "quiet until tomorrow"), never a bare "stop"; a pause names the way back, "resume check-ins" turns them back on, and the Agenda shows a pause with a Resume button</li>
          <li>A reading or watch list that comes back empty when refreshed keeps what it had, so a site that changed or half loaded no longer empties it</li>
          <li>Routines go on as their natural parts (a half hour each, keeping their steps), and the morning message lists the whole day, one plain line each with its time: each part with its steps, the meals and other anchors, then what was put on</li>
          <li>Meals keep to a sensible window: replanning the day puts them in it first, and a meal goes later only when the day is full (and says why) or you ask</li>
          <li>Before the morning reset clears the Today note, it files what's done into your Done note, then writes Today in one safe step: a ticked line can't disappear</li>
          <li>Only a real signal makes an item fixed to its time (a calendar event, or a time you gave); errands no longer stick at odd minutes</li>
          <li>An item whose time went by with nothing said waits under Not marked yet, with Done and Skip, never under Done today</li>
          <li>Dragging or stretching an item in the Timeline over a fixed one keeps both, and so does asking the assistant to stretch it (the same item, not a new copy); a fixed item that's already happened no longer blocks its time</li>
          <li>A routine at a set time every day never stops anything being set across it, by you or by the assistant: it keeps its time and shows alongside</li>
          <li>A change to your lists that Joplin cut off halfway is finished cleanly when Joplin is back, and one that fails never holds up the others; rewording or splitting a private item keeps the detail the cloud model never saw; and your own words in brackets in a list item are never mistaken for a hidden detail</li>
          <li>Private notes are hidden from the cloud model from the very first sorting of your lists at setup, and a line added to a private note later is hidden before the next sorting</li>
          <li>On the Day page, a calendar event past its time with nothing said waits under Not marked yet instead of vanishing, and items not marked on another day can be marked there</li>
          <li>A new deadline you set for a repeating item becomes the day its later deadlines follow</li>
          <li>A conversation's title in the chat is your own first words (or the day, before you've said anything), never one of Thirties' own instructions; titles already stored that way are put right</li>
          <li>Time on a project ("Garden app dev") never rolls over, whatever list it's on; "Pick up meds" is a one-off, not a daily thing</li>
          <li>What a replan can't fit waits for tomorrow instead of being deleted, a change that fails never half-happens, and a time you gave keeps its minutes when it's moved</li>
          <li>Long or marked-up list lines go on Today by their short title; a done item doesn't leave an earlier copy asking about it; old plan pins expire; a routine never goes on as one opaque item; and done times are only ones you said (or Thirties' estimate)</li>
          <li>Thirties now tells you when one of your memory notes covers something it handles by itself, so you can remove it: after each update it's marked "Thirties already does this by itself. Remove this note?" in Profile, Memory, and nothing is removed without your tap</li>
          <li>An agent's work-log note is planned as time on its project</li>
          <li>Every reply that changes your plan ends with what's next: what's on now, the next few things, anything the change moved, and what's not marked yet today, one line each, in the app and on Telegram</li>
          <li>An item you mark done again after taking the done back is ticked in Joplin again, and taking a done back unticks the item on its own list as well as on Today</li>
          <li>Thirties' own assistant runs privately on your computer (Gemma), and it isn't ready yet, so it's behind a developer switch; until it is, Thirties runs in debug mode on Gemini as a stand-in, which works as before</li>
        </ul>
      </description>
    </release>
    <release version="0.2.1" date="2026-11-11" type="development">
      <description>
        <p>Dropping an item no longer changes your lists:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>A drop only stops an item rolling over: it stays on its list in Joplin, unticked, and can be planned again; the Archive in Before today keeps the record</li>
          <li>An item is taken off its list only when you ask for that outright ("archive it", "delete it")</li>
          <li>Items dropped earlier, when a drop still took them off their lists, are offered back once in Before today</li>
        </ul>
      </description>
    </release>
    <release version="0.2.0" date="2026-11-08" type="development">
      <description>
        <p>Mornings that start when you do, and a day that simply is the plan:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>One morning message, written fresh each day, at your wake-up time or at sunrise, optionally with the day's items; nothing else is sent until you reply</li>
          <li>Your reply is the morning: the day is built then, as the plan, with no approval step; the one question left is what doesn't fit today</li>
          <li>The morning routine goes on from when you're actually up, a part per half hour, alongside work, in a block it fits</li>
          <li>What's left from before takes effect by itself: today's items go on today's schedule, the rest wait for a day they fit, and proposed drops are applied after the day unless you say otherwise</li>
          <li>Agenda: a Before today tab beside Today (keep, drop, done, and an archive you can undrop from); the Free row and each routine show their time in the time column</li>
          <li>Dropped items are ticked and filed in Joplin in a monthly Dropped note beside the Done notes; undropping puts them back where they were</li>
          <li>Items show a short title everywhere; a long line's details stay out of messages and expand in the Agenda</li>
          <li>Check-ins come at their own time, or not at all</li>
          <li>Other people's calendar events stay out of the schedule sent in chat and Telegram</li>
          <li>Telegram lists are one item per line, without bullets</li>
          <li>A Joplin change that can't go through is set aside after a few tries, instead of being retried forever</li>
          <li>The assistant no longer says it can't make changes</li>
        </ul>
      </description>
    </release>
    <release version="0.1.0" date="2026-10-30" type="development">
      <description>
        <p>The day list, and an assistant you can trust with your lists:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Each Joplin note has a role (routine, today, list, project, reference) and each item an urgency</li>
          <li>A Today note: the day's list, linked to the lists its items came from</li>
          <li>The plan and the record kept apart: replan on request, nothing assumed done</li>
          <li>How often Thirties messages you: off, light, medium or high</li>
          <li>A Day page (now, next, not reported, done today) beside the timeline</li>
          <li>Items keep their real times; the day is shown in half-hour blocks; fixed things never move</li>
          <li>Only the exact item is ever changed in Joplin, and what's done is never offered again</li>
          <li>"Remind me to…" puts the thing on your plan, with a ping when it's time</li>
          <li>Telegram replies are retried, and start-of-block notices are kept in the chat</li>
        </ul>
      </description>
    </release>
    <release version="0.0.1" date="2026-10-17" type="development">
      <description>
        <p>First alpha of Thirties:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Setup assistant: location, usual week, assistant, Joplin, sync and phone</li>
          <li>Day planning by conversation, told in thirties</li>
          <li>Joplin lists and GNOME calendars, with write-back to Joplin</li>
          <li>Reminders, check-ins and start messages, on the desktop and by Telegram</li>
          <li>Morning rollover of unfinished items, and statistics from what actually happened</li>
          <li>Runs in the background, with autostart</li>
        </ul>
      </description>
    </release>
  </releases>

</component>
